		<figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic">
			The Stripe Reader M2, ready for a contactless test card.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
</div>

2. **A Swift iPad app, and I don't know Swift.** I'd previously used agents to build apps I could comfortably understand myself. This time, not so much. Or more truthfully, not at all. So I really needed to ensure I had clear requirements and tests, and that my agents could drive Xcode independently.

<figure class="my-8 mx-auto max-w-2xl">
	<ZoomableImage
		src={ipadScreenshot}
		alt="Screenshot of the Swift iPad point-of-sale app"
		class="rounded-lg shadow-lg w-full"
	/>
	<figcaption class="text-center text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic">
		The Swift iPad app — the checkout screen the toddler actually uses.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

3. **I wanted to achieve end to end spec-driven development.** In my [prior attempt at using SpecKit](https://fintechnick.com/projects/speckit-dev) I couldn't quite get a workflow working that generated a functional application — my agent kept getting confused and lost in fruitless iteration. I wanted to do better this time.

4. **Mobile first workflow.** Sitting down at a computer outside of work for an extended time isn't something I can do often, but I do have spare pockets of time while I'm on the go. I'd already embraced asynchronous development with [Google Jules](https://jules.google.com/), but that didn't have access to all the tools on my desktop, was sometimes slow — taking hours to do a single request — and was designed to be asynchronous, so didn't work well for workflows where I wanted to iterate with the agent. I wanted a setup as similar as possible to my desktop: same tools, same performance, just accessible on the go.

So how did it go? Well, better than I expected. The hardware worked. After a bit of custom setup I wrangled Xcode into my Linux container agent workflow. I finally got a well structured and validated spec process that even worked well with the cheaper DeepSeek V4 model compared to my prior use of Gemini Pro. And I did most of it while away from my desktop. It was also a lot of fun, and I think it showed me even more what building software will look like with agents.

Right. Let's dig in and build a store. Target customer: a three-year-old.

## Hey Toy Store; Meet Stripe

A store. An iPad, a Tera Mini wireless scanner, a Stripe Reader M2, a test credit card, and toy food with printed QR* (footnote here about starting with bar codes and finding how difficult it is for little hands to line them up with the scanner) codes. The product owner is three years old. So is the QA lead. So is the most demanding user.

Here's the intended flow: user scans food items, each item then pops up on the iPad with a photo and a price — $1, $5, etc... Tap the card on the card reader and behind the scenes we run through the payments flow and boom: fireworks, music, much excitement. Then "Go Again".

