Underneath this workflow, we've got an e-commerce stack:

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
%%{init: {'theme':'dark'}}%%
graph TD
    subgraph ClientDevices [Client Devices and Peripherals]
        iPadApp["iPad POS App&#10;Swift / iOS"]
        BarcodeScanner["Tera Mini Barcode Scanner&#10;Bluetooth HID Mode"]
        StripeReader["Stripe Reader M2&#10;Bluetooth BLE Mode"]
        AveryPrinter["Avery Label Printer&#10;AirPrint / Browser Print"]
    end

    subgraph ExternalServices [External Integrations]
        StripeAPI["Stripe API&#10;Terminal and Payments"]
        AppAttest["Apple App Attest Service&#10;Device Verification"]
    end

    subgraph EdgeBackend [Cloudflare Workers Edge Platform]
        RustWorker["Payments and Admin API Worker&#10;Rust / Wasm"]
        subgraph CloudflareStorage [Cloudflare Storage]
            D1Db["D1 Relational DB&#10;SQLite Transactions"]
            KVStore["KV Namespace&#10;Inventory Data"]
        end
    end

    subgraph AdminControl [Admin Portal]
        AdminUI["Admin Web App&#10;www.fintechnick.com"]
    end

    BarcodeScanner -->|Bluetooth HID / Keystrokes| iPadApp
    StripeReader <-->|Bluetooth BLE / Stripe Terminal SDK| iPadApp
    iPadApp <-->|HTTPS REST / App Attest Signed| RustWorker
    AdminUI <-->|HTTPS REST / Auth API Key| RustWorker

    RustWorker <-->|Cloudflare Binding| D1Db
    RustWorker <-->|Cloudflare Binding| KVStore

    RustWorker <-->|HTTPS / REST API| StripeAPI
    RustWorker -->|HTTPS / Validation Requests| AppAttest
    AdminUI -->|Browser Render / CSS @media print| AveryPrinter
```

</div>

There's three main pieces:

1. **Swift iPad app** (the scanner connects in Bluetooth keyboard-wedge mode, meaning it mimics a keyboard, while the M2 scanner integrates through the [Stripe Terminal SDK](https://docs.stripe.com/terminal) rather than an iOS Bluetooth connection, and every request is signed with Apple App Attest so only my iPad can talk to the backend);

2. **Rust worker on Cloudflare** that the iPad communicates with (put footnote here that I was inspired to use Rust after Wes McKinney's good point that with agents (https://wesmckinney.com/blog/agent-ergonomics/) there's value in picking tools that work well with agents, if not humans. With Rust giving higher performance and better feedback on compile errors). The worker issues Stripe Terminal tokens that lets the Stripe Terminal SDK connect to the card reader, creates and captures `card_present` [PaymentIntents](https://docs.stripe.com/api/payment_intents) that represent the payment lifecycle, and keeps photos and prices of inventory in Cloudflare's key-value storage [KV](https://developers.cloudflare.com/kv/) with a transaction log in Cloudflare's Serverless SQL (SQLite) [D1](https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/);

3. **Admin website** for inventory photo upload, pricing, QR generation, label printing, and a bit of analytics.

