If you want all the details, the [repo's specs](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs) have the full OpenAPI, D1 schema, and KV layout. Here I want to talk about two things that made the whole project possible that for me, were new, powerful, and I think generally applicable; the spec-driven process and the phone-first workflow.

## Spec-Driven Process: The Harness

In my prior [Spec Kit article](/projects/speckit-dev) I tried to embrace Spec-Driven development and it all went a bit wrong. I struggled with specs that were hard to update once code existed, and I created a mess of branch conflicts. Even on a flagship model — Gemini Pro at the time — the agent couldn't manage the full scope of the project. But it wasn't the model that was the problem, it was the structure I was using around it. With a revisit to the concept, a new process fixed my problems:

### A structured artifact pipeline

Instead of running a workflow that went **spec file → plan file → code**, my pipeline was now **spec → artifacts → validation → plan → code**.

1. **spec** — this remained nearly unchanged to my prior approach. I created a textual description of what my project needed to do, along with a `constitution.md` outlining various constraints that the project needed to uphold. The spec itself was generated with the guidance `this is a _design doc, not a full coding spec_. A senior engineer reading it should feel confident they could implement the whole thing, making judgment calls on the details.`
2. **artifacts** — here's where things differed. We generated structured artifacts that described different aspects of the project, specifically: state diagrams, message flows, UI mocks, deployment topology, APIs, data models, and capacity models.
3. **validation** — to validate we took the artifacts and cross-checked them against each other for consistency by generating a dependency map using the [specdag](https://github.com/japorto100/specdag) tool. This step ensured that there were no dangling dependencies such as a UI mock that wasn't referenced in any message flows, or any circular dependencies. Although we can't guarantee we didn't miss any requirements, it was a step that did give me confidence that the design was self-consistent.
4. **plan** — them the implementation plan is generated _off the validated artifacts_. This ensures we have a higher quality result than if we just generate a plan from the spec.
5. **scaffold-project + generate-code** — once validated my approach was similar to past efforts, but one change was that I first scaffolded the project using [genproj](https://fintechnick.com/projects/genproj), a personal code generation tool I built that creates all the config and setup for my favorite frameworks and tools. genproj creates templates for things like my CI/CD, secrets management, devcontainers, code quality checks and cloud deployment scripts. This scaffold meant that the implementation generation could focus on the core logic.

For those interested in the details, the workflow is codified as a set of [goose recipes here](https://github.com/nickbrett1/goose-recipes).

And here's the artifact tree this produced for stripe-toddler — every artifact cross-checked against the others:

- **[`spec/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec)** — the validated artifact tree
  - **[`topology/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/topology)**
    - [`component-graph.mmd`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/topology/component-graph.mmd) — system nodes and DAG
    - [`deployment-targets.md`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/topology/deployment-targets.md) — infrastructure mapping
  - **[`api/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/api)**
    - [`worker-openapi.yaml`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/api/worker-openapi.yaml) — the Rust worker's API
    - [`swift-protocols.md`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/api/swift-protocols.md) — iOS interface contracts
  - **[`data-architecture/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/data-architecture)**
    - [`d1-schema.sql`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/data-architecture/d1-schema.sql) — SQLite analytics schema
    - [`kv-layout.json`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/data-architecture/kv-layout.json) — inventory access patterns
    - [`domain-models.rs`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/data-architecture/domain-models.rs) — core structs
  - **[`flows/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/flows)**
    - [`checkout-sequence.md`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/flows/checkout-sequence.md) — step-by-step transaction walkthrough
    - [`payment-state-machine.md`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/flows/payment-state-machine.md) — intent and hardware lifecycle
  - **[`ui/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/ui)**
    - [`wireframes/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/ui/wireframes) — low-fidelity Excalidraw mocks
  - **[`capacity/`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/tree/main/specs/spec/capacity)**
    - [`load-model.md`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/specs/spec/capacity/load-model.md) — sizing, retention, IOPS

And here's the payment state machine — a nice validated artifact that helped ensure the code would cover all the necessary cases:

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
%%{init: {'theme':'dark'}}%%
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> WaitingForScan : App launch and initialization

    WaitingForScan --> CartActive : Barcode scanned and verified in KV
    WaitingForScan --> ErrorScreen : Network error or barcode not in KV

    CartActive --> CartActive : Additional items scanned
    CartActive --> WaitingForScan : All items removed from cart
    CartActive --> ReaderSyncing : Tap the Pay button

    ReaderSyncing --> AwaitingCardTap : Terminal token retrieved and intent created
    ReaderSyncing --> ErrorScreen : Stripe API error or backend timeout

    AwaitingCardTap --> ProcessingPayment : Card tapped successfully
    AwaitingCardTap --> ErrorScreen : Card read timeout or card rejected

    ProcessingPayment --> Celebrating : Capture endpoint returns HTTP 200
    ProcessingPayment --> ErrorScreen : Capture fails on Stripe or database write error

    Celebrating --> WaitingForScan : Tap the Go Again button
    ErrorScreen --> WaitingForScan : Tap the Dismiss button
```

</div>

And here's the artifact for the checkout flow as a message sequence:

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
%%{init: {'theme':'dark'}}%%
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Toddler as Toddler
    participant iPad as iPad App (Swift)
    participant Scanner as Barcode Scanner (HID)
    participant Reader as Stripe Reader M2 (BLE)
    participant Backend as Rust Worker (Cloudflare)
    participant KV as Cloudflare KV
    participant D1 as D1 SQLite DB
    participant Stripe as Stripe API

    Note over Toddler, Scanner: Scan phase
    Toddler->>Scanner: Scans toy barcode
    Scanner->>iPad: Keystrokes and carriage return
    iPad->>Backend: GET inventory by barcode (attested)
    Backend->>KV: Fetch item details
    KV-->>Backend: Name, price, photo URL
    Backend-->>iPad: Item details

    Note over Toddler, Reader: Payment phase
    Toddler->>iPad: Taps the Pay button
    iPad->>Backend: POST payment-intent
    Backend->>Stripe: POST /v1/payment_intents (card_present)
    Stripe-->>Backend: client_secret and intent ID
    Backend-->>iPad: client_secret and intent ID
    iPad->>Reader: collectPaymentMethod(client_secret)
    Toddler->>Reader: Taps test card
    Reader-->>iPad: Authorized intent

    Note over iPad, D1: Capture phase
    iPad->>Backend: POST capture
    Backend->>Stripe: POST /v1/payment_intents/pi_xxx/capture
    Stripe-->>Backend: Capture succeeded
    Backend->>D1: INSERT INTO transactions
    D1-->>Backend: Confirmed
    Backend-->>iPad: Transaction ID

    Note over Toddler, iPad: Celebration phase
    iPad->>iPad: Fireworks, guitar band, sound
    Toddler->>iPad: Taps Go Again
```

</div>

Beyond having a clear definition of the expected behavior of the project, this also greatly decreased the cost and risk of making changes. Instead of the change rippling through a mountain of agent created code, it could be made first in these small, relatively cheap artifacts, then validated. After which it was a more straightforward translation step to change the code.

## Mobile first development

Having a process for building the application was only part of the story, I simply didn't have enough concentrated time in front of my desktop computer to get it done. I needed to make development possible for when I was on the go. I'd previously been using [Google Jules](https://jules.google.com/), an asynchronous web-based tool for fixing bugs or making small enhancements with agents, but I needed something much more powerful, full access to my desktop toolchain from my phone. Here's what I built:

**One devcontainer, one view.** The Rust backend and the Swift iPad app both live in a single VSCode devcontainer (stripe-toddler). However the admin screen is part of a different project (ftn), and so live in a different VSCode devcontainer.

This was relatively simple to expose, I could ensure each container was added to my [tailscale](https://tailscale.com/) VPN network when it started, and then using [blink](https://blink.sh/) I could use a mobile shell to remote in.

One challenge was how to get access to Xcode, it can't run in a container and runs as a native application on my host (Mac) desktop.

For this I took advantage of the relatively new [MCP support in Xcode 26.3](https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/02/xcode-26-point-3-unlocks-the-power-of-agentic-coding/). However this wasn't enough, because I ran my development environment in my Linux dev container which didn't have direct access to my host Mac. To bridge from my dev container, there needed to be two additional pieces:

1. A small proxy running in my dev container ([`mcp-sse-proxy.cjs`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/.agents/mcp-sse-proxy.cjs) to connect over HTTP to the mac host.

2. Another small proxy running on my mac-studio ([`xcode-mcp-server.js`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/stripe-toddler/blob/main/mcp/xcode-mcp-server.js) maps HTTP requests back to stdio. This is then run using launchctl.

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
%%{init: {'theme':'dark'}}%%
flowchart LR
    Phone["Phone&#10;Blink Shell"] -->|"mosh over Tailscale"| DevContainer["Linux devcontainer&#10;VS Code + agent + tmux"]
    DevContainer -->|HTTP| Proxy["mcp-sse-proxy.cjs&#10;(in the container)"]
    Proxy -->|"HTTP over Tailscale"| Mac["mac-studio&#10;xcode-mcp-server.js (PM2)"]
    Mac -->|stdio| Xcode["Xcode 26.3&#10;native MCP server"]
    Xcode -->|"edit / build / test / launch"| iPad["iPad app"]
```

</div>

With this my agent in my dev container I can edit all the xcode project files, build, run tests and even launch the iPad app itself all from my agent in my dev container. Of course from a phone I couldn't use the iPad app, but I could get a lot done before final acceptance testing at my desktop.

The final benefit with this setup is that I can seamlessly move between my phone and my desktop. I have the same tmux session on my phone as my desktop, when I sit down at my desktop I see the last thing I typed on my mobile, and vice-versa.

**Seamless setup**

Having a setup that let me access a container seamlessly when it's up and running is useful, but I wanted to go further and ensure I could launch a vscode dev container from my phone. Not only is this useful for starting a new project, but it also protected me against machine reboots on my desktop.

For this, I created a simple script ([`devopen`](https://github.com/nickbrett1/devopen)) which can clone code from any repo, build and start its respective devcontainer and open the VS Code window.

And of course, I also made sure my Mac would auto-login if it rebooted, and as a final tool to use in an emergency I have the excellent [Jump Desktop](https://jumpdesktop.com/) remote desktop software.

## The Magical Moment

<div class="mermaid-container">

```mermaid
%%{init: {'theme':'dark'}}%%
flowchart LR
    Observe["1. Observe&#10;Phone: the celebration is weak"] --> Instruct["2. Instruct&#10;Ask the agent on the phone"]
    Instruct --> Bridge["3. Bridge&#10;MCP proxy to mac-studio to Xcode"]
    Bridge --> Build["4. Build&#10;edit to rebuild to tests"]
    Build --> Verified["5. Verified&#10;iPad reloads, mid-demo"]
```

</div>

This all came together in a quite magical moment as I was trying the app with my test user for the first time.

It became clear that the celebration screen was not up to the expected quality standards, with not enough fireworks and no sound.

In the moment here's what I did:

1. **Phone** — [Blink Shell](https://blink.sh/), mosh into the VSCode devcontainer over Tailscale. Mosh keeps the session alive.
2. **Agent** — I ask the agent in my vscode terminal to make the celebration more exciting.
3. **One workspace** — the agent can understand the whole stack, with both the Rust backend and the Swift iPad app (mounted from the mac-studio host).
4. **MCP bridge** — the agent reaches the custom MCP wrapper on the Mac host (`mcp-sse-proxy.cjs` → `xcode-native` MCP at `mac-studio:9876/sse`).
5. **Xcode** — the wrapper drives Xcode end-to-end: edit → rebuild → run tests, all automated
6. **The iPad, at the other side of the house** — reloads with the new celebration, mid-demo, in response to the critical user feedback.
7. **Updated Celebration** — all within about 30 seconds

In a pre-agentic world, when giving demos this is a "note it down and fix it tonight" moment — a task that goes on a list, gets reviewed and prioritized, and ships tomorrow at the earliest. We've now got a **30-second loop**: observation → instruction → shipped → verified, all while the user is watching. That was amazing to see in action.

## Takeaways

Three things I learned from this:

1. **Artifacts before code.** This was perhaps in hindsight obvious, a robust software design process has diagrams and review documents. But working out how to integrate that notion with agents took me some time. But with typed, cross-validated artifacts — state diagrams, message flows, UI mocks — sitting between the spec and the plan, coupled with validation, I saw a significant improvement in the quality of the final output.

2. **Phone access unlocked bandwidth** Getting the ability to do real development work from my phone turned this from a project that would probably never have even started to something that could be chipped away at in the free moments I had. Each step was small, but in the end, led to a complete project.

3. **Make iteration a phone-speed loop.** Having a complete end-to-end loop from my phone, through my dev environment with automated deployment gave an insight into how fast we can now iterate on software. I wasn't expected this at all, but a "note it down and fix it tonight" process became a 30-second iteration, and possible from my phone.

